  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nPotent upper level shortwave and attendant surface low will\ncontinue translating southeast across New England through\ndaybreak, and off the New England coast during the day. Tight\nlow level pressure gradient and strong low level jet around the\nwestern side of the low will move across the region this\nmorning. Latest forecast soundings suggest potential for wind\ngusts to reach 30-40 mph, especially within the Hudson River\nValley due to channeled flow. Strongest gusts are expected this\nmorning, with gradual diminishing trend this afternoon.\n\nBand of showers wrapping around the western side of the low will\npivot south and east across most areas through mid morning,\nalthough showers may remain east of the Mohawk Valley. Period of\nsteady rain expected across southern VT, and for elevations\nmainly above 2000 feet, strong dynamical and wet bulb cooling \nshould allow rain to mix with, or change to snow by daybreak and\ncontinue until mid morning. For these higher elevations, up to\nan inch or two of snow is possible on colder surfaces this \nmorning.\n\nShowers should decrease in coverage from west to east later this\nmorning, though may persist across portions of western New\nEngland, especially southeast VT, until around or shortly after\nnoontime.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBelow normal temperatures will continue today through tonight.\nTemps will only slowly rise through the 40s and lower 50s during\nthis morning, before reaching late afternoon highs in the 50s to\nlower 60s, coolest across western New England.\n\nClearing skies and decreasing wind tonight will allow temps to\ndrop off into the mid/upper 30s across the southern Adirondacks,\nsouthern VT and eastern Catskills where patchy frost will be\npossible. There is a bit of uncertainty regarding whether even\ncolder temps will occur, as the main surface high will remain\noff to our west, which may allow for a light breeze to persist\nand limit overall cooling potential. Should winds become calm,\nfrost and colder temps would be more widespread in these areas.\nElsewhere, expect lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nLingering upper level trough/cold pool will promote additional \nchances for showers Sunday through at least Tuesday, especially\nduring each afternoon due to diurnal heating. There could be \nenough shallow instability for a few embedded low-topped \nthunderstorms, especially Monday afternoon. Although some small \nhail/gusty winds can not be ruled out, any storms should be \nisolated and below severe thresholds.",
